# Development Rules & Workflow

**Project:** Tigrinya / Amharic Learning App
**Platform:** Native iOS
**Tech:** Swift + SwiftUI
**Repository:** Git
**Project Management:** Linear

---

# Core Principles

Apply these principles to all development:

1. **Simplicity first** — Prefer simple over complex solutions
2. **Modular architecture** — Build components that are loosely coupled and highly cohesive
3. **Testable code** — Write code that can be easily tested
4. **Performance awareness** — Consider performance implications from the start
5. **Security by default** — Build security into every layer
6. **Stunning premium UI** — Every screen must feel polished, delightful, and user-friendly

---

# 1. Branching Strategy

Use a **structured Git workflow** inspired by GitFlow.

## Main Branches

```
main
staging
develop
```

### main

Production-ready code.

Rules:

• Always stable
• Only CI can deploy from here
• No direct commits
• Only merge from **staging**

---

### staging

Pre-production testing environment.

Used for:

• QA testing
• TestFlight builds
• integration testing

---

### develop

Active development branch.

All feature branches merge here first.

---

# 2. Supporting Branches

## Feature Branch

Used for building features.

Naming rule:

```
feature/<ticket-number>-<short-description>
```

Example:

```
feature/LIN-12-fidel-lesson-screen
feature/LIN-25-audio-pronunciation
```

---

## Fix Branch

For bugs found during development.

```
fix/<ticket-number>-<description>
```

Example:

```
fix/LIN-34-audio-playback-crash
```

---

## Hotfix Branch

For urgent production fixes.

```
hotfix/<issue>
```

Example:

```
hotfix/login-crash
```

Hotfix flow:

```
main → hotfix → main + staging
```

---

# 3. Pull Request Rules

All code must go through **Pull Requests (PRs)**.

Rules:

• Minimum **1 approval**
• All CI checks must pass
• Code review required

PR checklist:

```
- Tests written
- Code formatted
- No warnings
- Documentation updated
```

---

# 4. Commit Message Standards

Use **Conventional Commits**.

Structure:

```
type(scope): description
```

Examples:

```
feat(lesson): add fidel alphabet lesson screen
fix(audio): resolve playback delay
refactor(progress): simplify progress tracking
test(quiz): add unit tests for quiz logic
```

Commit types:

| Type     | Purpose                  |
| -------- | ------------------------ |
| feat     | new feature              |
| fix      | bug fix                  |
| refactor | code restructuring       |
| test     | testing                  |
| docs     | documentation            |
| perf     | performance improvements |

---

# 5. CI/CD Pipeline

CI/CD automatically checks code quality and builds the app.

Recommended tools:

• GitHub Actions
• Xcode Cloud
• Fastlane

CI pipeline steps:

```
1. Install dependencies
2. Lint code
3. Run unit tests
4. Run UI tests
5. Build app
6. Performance checks
```

Deployment pipeline:

```
develop → internal build
staging → TestFlight
main → App Store
```

---

# 6. Code Quality Rules

Use automated tools.

### Linter

Recommended:

SwiftLint

Rules:

• no unused imports
• max line length
• consistent naming

---

### Formatter

SwiftFormat

Ensures consistent formatting.

---

# 7. Architecture Rules

Use **MVVM architecture**.

Structure:

```
App
 ├── Features
 │    ├── Lessons
 │    ├── Flashcards
 │    ├── Quizzes
 │
 ├── Core
 │    ├── Networking
 │    ├── Storage
 │    ├── Audio
 │
 ├── UI
 │    ├── Components
 │    ├── Theme
 │
 ├── Models
 ├── ViewModels
 ├── Views
```

---

# 8. Code Standards

General rules:

• Functions under **40 lines**
• One responsibility per class
• Use dependency injection
• Avoid global state

Naming conventions:

```
PascalCase → Types
camelCase → variables/functions
UPPER_CASE → constants
```

Example:

```
LessonView
lessonViewModel
MAX_LESSONS
```

---

# 9. Performance Rules

Performance targets:

App launch:

```
< 2 seconds
```

Memory usage:

```
< 150MB
```

Frame rate:

```
60 FPS UI animations
```

Rules:

• lazy loading for lessons
• cache audio locally
• avoid blocking main thread

---

# 10. Testing Strategy

Three levels of testing.

## Unit Tests

Test business logic.

Example:

```
Quiz scoring
Lesson progress
Flashcard algorithm
```

Framework:

XCTest

---

## UI Tests

Automated interface testing.

Example:

```
Lesson navigation
Quiz interaction
Flashcard flipping
```

---

## Integration Tests

Test combined systems.

Example:

```
audio + lessons
progress tracking
```

---

# 11. Performance Testing

Use:

Xcode Instruments

Monitor:

• CPU usage
• memory leaks
• animation performance

---

# 12. Scrum Development Process

Use **2-week sprints**.

Sprint structure:

```
Planning
Development
Review
Retrospective
```

---

## Sprint Planning

Tasks come from Linear issues.

Example backlog:

```
LIN-12 Alphabet lesson UI
LIN-13 Flashcard engine
LIN-14 Audio system
```

Each task must include:

• description
• acceptance criteria
• design references

---

# 13. Definition of Done

A task is done when:

```
✓ Code implemented
✓ Tests written
✓ Code reviewed
✓ CI passed
✓ Documentation updated
✓ Merged to develop
```

---

# 14. Release Process

Release flow:

```
feature → develop
develop → staging
staging → main
main → App Store
```

Release steps:

1. merge staging → main
2. tag version

Example:

```
v1.0.0
```

3. deploy to App Store

---

# 15. Security Rules

Protect user data.

Rules:

• no secrets in repo
• use secure storage
• encrypt sensitive data

Use:

Keychain for tokens.

---

# 16. Documentation

All major components must include:

```
README
Architecture explanation
Setup instructions
```

Docs should cover:

• project setup
• development workflow
• deployment steps

---

# 17. Project Management

Use Linear.

Workflow states:

```
Backlog
Planned
In Progress
Review
Done
```

Issue template:

```
Title
Description
Acceptance criteria
Technical notes
```

---

# 18. Versioning Strategy

Use **Semantic Versioning**.

Structure:

```
MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH
```

Example:

```
1.0.0
```

Meaning:

| Version | Meaning      |
| ------- | ------------ |
| 1.x.x   | new features |
| x.1.x   | improvements |
| x.x.1   | bug fixes    |

---

# 19. Cursor Development Rules

Cursor should:

• generate **modular SwiftUI code**
• follow **MVVM architecture**
• write **unit tests automatically**
• respect **lint rules**

Every generated file must include:

```
- documentation comments
- testable logic
```

